In article <7vae9u$djg$1@nserve1.acs.ucalgary.ca>,
  patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii) wrote:
> No, this understanding is wrong. Placing a port name in /etc/services
> has no effect apart from giving that port a convenient symbolic name,
> which every individual program is free to use (or ignore). The only
> way
> to actually reserve a port number is to bind() to it - in which case
> it will not be available for other processes.

Interesting. :*|

The bind-lock would only be active while the process is running, though,
correct? If I understand then, we'd need to have a 'sleeper' process
bind the port while the server is down, and then kill that process
before restarting the server.
