In article <7v45vs$smc$1@nnrp1.deja.com> esafran@aluar.com.ar writes:
>NTPQ output follows:
>
>B# ntpq -p
>remote      refid     st
>-------------------------
>* LOCAL(1)  LOCAL(1)  3
>  A         B         5
>
>A# ntpq -p
>remote      refid     st
>-------------------------
>  LOCAL(1)  LOCAL(1)  3
>* B         LOCAL(1)  4

As explained in other postings in this thread, this is not a reliable
configuration - note that A has its own local clock at a lower stratum
than B; it may switch over to its local clock at any time and stay
there, with the result that the nodes start drifting apart. Also, you
should always "fudge" the local clocks to have a higher stratum than the
default of 3 (e.g. 10, 12), to make sure they don't interfere with
*real* time sources. (You may not have any now, but if you one day get
them this may come back and bite you, and possibly others.)

--Per Hedeland
per@erix.ericsson.se



