Robert Koehler wrote:
> 
> Bob Kaplow (kaplow_r@eisner.decus.org) wrote:
> : Forgive me for stepping outside my VMS knowledge base and asking a Tru64Unix
> : V4.0D question...
> [...]
> 
> : What does this "BOOTDEVICE_PARSER: UNKNOWN PROTOCOL NAME 'SCSI'" error mean?
> : What do I need to do to Tru64Unix when I move its system disk from one
> : controller to another to make it bootable?
> 
> You may need to rebuild the kernel.  Some UNIX kernels can get the swap
> device name built into them and refuse to boot if you change the physical
> name of the disk.  You should be able to boot the generic kernel in any
> case (/genvmunix).  There should be a boot flag which allows you to
> specify the disk, parition, and file name for the kernel.

boot -fi genvmunix (if bootdef_dev is set to the correct boot device)

When inside unix, look at any swap device related statements in
/etc/fstab, /etc/rc.config and the link /sbin/swapdefault
They need to point at the right devices & partitions.
Do a "mount -u -w /" to make / writeable if it's not allready.

The kernel also needs a doconfig if you've moved around or added any
controller cards.

If your device ID's has changed and you run AdvFS, you'll also need to
relink everything in /etc/fdmns
Declared AdvFS file domains for devices that doesn't exist will also
make the machine crash. Do a rmfdmn on those, or be safe and just move
them away from /etc/fdmns

If you run LSM it's a bit more tricky...


l8r/Jspr



