  esafran@aluar.com.ar wrote:
> I have a Digital Unix cluster of two hosts and I want to synchronize
> their clocks. I use only the local clocks of the cluster and my
> question refers about the association about them.
>
> * Must they be symmetric active peers, both configured as local
> reference clock?
> * Must they be the same as previous but one of them chosed as "prefer"
> server?
> * Must one of them be configured as time server and the other as
client?
>
> I have tried the three posibilities and all of them appears to work
> fine, but I am not sure wich is the best, or if there is another one
> better.

AFAIK, These 3 possibilities are almost same. your 2 servers will be
synchronised together, but will drift from UTC sooner or later.
I'd choose to declare them as peers, and each with local reference clock
also. I guess that both ntp daemons will elect the best local clock
among 2 servers, and synchronise to it.

If you choose one of other 2 solutions, you actually decide arbitraly
which local clock to use, and you have 50% chance to choose the worse
one.

I think you really should consider to have an external synchronisation
source...


Jerome



