I got the following feedback from a couple of my colleagues who spent a long
Sunday getting a GS60E up on Tru64UNIX due to the following:

On the GS60E platform, you need to pass a parameter to the kernel when 
booting. The parameter is "ws_graphics=3D3". If this parameter is not 
passed, the system will boot but will not be usable due to the xlogin 
procedure consuming all available cpus. You can log in if this happens,  but
you need to allow 3 to 5 seconds between each key stroke - otherwise 
characters will be lost. Once logged in, type the following:

/sbin/init.d/xlogin stop

This will terminate the xlogin procedure and return the system to a usable 
state. However, there will be no way to do a full screen CDE session. 
Individual X windows can still be started and sent to your workstation via 
Exceed.

To boot the system with the necessary parameter, use one of the steps 
below:

A: at the >>> prompt type: b -fl i

Once the system gets to the next prompt, type: vmunix ws_graphics=3D3 and 
hit return. The system will now boot to single user mode. Once at single 
user mode type: init 3. The system will now proceed to multiuser mode.

B: at the >>> prompt type: set boot_file "vmunix ws_graphics=3D3"  (be sure
to use the quotes with this command)

Once back at the >>> prompt boot as you normally would. Once this flag is 
set, you should not have to reset it in the future. To verify that it is 
set type "show boot*" at the >>> prompt. Look for: boot_file and verify 
that it has the correct value.

	Bob Kaplow				HUG not NUG



