From: peter@abbnm.com (Peter da Silva)
Subject: DEQ: get a licensing clue... Re: Killer deal on DS10s
Date: 1999/10/24
Message-ID: <7uv3th$eaq@web.nmti.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 539950039
References: <7u0b9a$g1g$1@nnrp1.deja.com> <bdoKON2xNAnLYHWsrd9KEGVLjdrZ@4ax.com> <rz7d7uacfyp.fsf@corton.inria.fr> <Bjz1B53B7RJW@malvm2.mala.bc.ca>
Organization: Network/development platform support, NMTI
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.osf1

The Digital UNIX licensing model is suicidal.

It is designed for an environment where there are not many copies of the
software sold, so each license is carefully graded based on some measure
of its value to the customer. The problem is that this makes it hard to
buy a license, and the license is non-portable... as you upgrade hardware,
you have to buy more licenses to go with the faster hardware.

This guarantees that there will never be many copies of the software sold.

The problem with this is that it's an operating system. Its value to the
customer is less related to the power of the hardware it's running on than
to the applications available for it. As time goes on, more and more of
the software vendors who write for it realise that it's a niche market, and
it will always be a nice market as long as it's licensed as a niche product,
so they have no incentive to continue supporting that version of their
software.

So as time goes on that license you have to keep paying for is worth less
and less, and because you're moving to faster hardware just because of
Moore's Law it's costing you more and more. So long as the Alpha remains
the fastest processor out there, there's going to be people who can justify
the license costs on those terms, but that's an ever-shrinking market...
and as software vendors drop the Alpha they're going to be forced to jump
ship simply because their mission critical software doesn't run on it any
more.

But we've been telling DEQ this for years, and watching it happen, and they
simply don't get it. I don't expect them to clue onto it now.

-- 
