I've heard a lot of legacy myths as well, or at least things that I've not
been able to duplicate or see.

: But, ADVFS offers wonderful advantages in being able to dynamically
: partition disks and re-arrange physical storage capacity as needs change. 
: We've been running ADVFS on our production machines for a couple years
: now, and at this point, I don't know how I ever survived without it.

The last company I worked for had been running AdvFS on servers and
workstations for 4 years with NO problems ever.  No corruption issues, no
downtime, no system panics.  Rock-solid (and well worth that for us, we had
power-problems in at least two of the facilities), wonderful features.  And
the servers we were using this on were running upwards of 150 GB of storage,
with anywhere up to 80 GB in one domain.  We were never even particularly
aggressive about balancing or patching.

I don't know whether our hands-off approach played into the fact that it
worked so well or not, but we never had any of the problems I've seen
related in this group.  Maybe we were lucky, don't know.  We'd been using
AdvFS since 3.2c IIRC, possibly even 3.0.  One thing we did do is as we
upgraded hardware and OS level, we did make use of extra disk space to build
fresh domains and move the data from the old to the new.  That may address
some issues that I've heard.  I know there is a conversion process in 5.0 to
the newer domain format, maybe that helps address this issue.

Chris

