> ... from Alan:
>
> Tru64 UNIX (Digital UNIX or DEC OSF/1) has two modes of page/swap
> space reservation.  In the default mode, nearly every page of
> virtual memory created has a page of swap space reserved for it,
> to ensure that if the page needs to be written out, there will
> be space for it.  The reservation does little more than adjust
> a counter with the actual allocation of space being held off
> until needed.
>
> If the amount of reserved space exceeds 90% of the available
> space, the kernel prints a warning to that affect.
>
> The other mode doesn't reserve space, but introduces the risk
> that there won't be page/swap space available if something
> needs it.  When that happens the kernel looks for processes
> to kill to free up their page/swap space.  In the right
> environments this other mode has advantages, but in the
> wrong environment opens the system to an easy denial of
> service attack.
> --
> Alan Rollow    alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com
