Lamont Granquist <lamontg@u.washington.edu> wrote:
: rsimpson@ewrcsdra.CutThisOut.demon.co.uk writes:
: How are work units being accepted by S@H if they haven't released the
: client?  The only possibility is that Compaq is internally distributing
: this client and those are Compaq work units.

Yes, this is exactly what is happening.  It doesn't need to be a revelation,
since Compaq have stated exactly the above in this very newsgroup.

: Just the fact that WUs are showing up on the webpage
: doesn't mean that S@H has blessed the client as being correct.  It means
: that someone has their hands on the client and is running it.

Wrong again!  When I client returns a WU, it indicates details such as the
platform on which it has been running.  Obviously, that is how the tables of
results per platform are created.  So, if S@H don't KNOW that the Tru64
clients generate valid results they can simply tell their servers not to
accept results coming from that particular platform.

How do we know that the servers can be programmed in this way?  Well, apart
from the fact that it would be a pretty crap server which couldn't be told
to reject certain clients, we know because it has been done in the past.  Do
you remember the Mac client which due to a bug tended to return all WUs
after only a few seconds of processing?  This caused chaos in the results,
and a lot of clearing up.  Rediculously low average CPU times from Mac users
don't appear any longer because those clients have been locked out and Mac
users forced to upgrade to the fixed version.

Yes, if one has access to the source, one can presumably forge the results
to appear to have come from a different platform.  Perhaps this has already
happened.  But producing a blazingly fast Tru64 client which pretended to be
running under (for example) Solaris (or even OSF for that matter) would not
give Compaq the publicity which presumably at least partly drives their
effort to produce these clients. 

: Personally, I'd be hoping that the delay in getting the new alpha clients
: up on the webpage has to do with the work that they're doing in making
: their setup more secure.  That certainly takes precedence over adding a
: client to the webpage.

Well, since the Tru64 clients started submitting large numbers of results,
the text version download page has been updated at least once (presumably to
adds some other clients).  And as I am getting bored with repeating, if they
don't want the hassle of updating the web page they could just bung it on
the FTP site.  That can't possibly have any effect on their security and
requires only the use of a single 'rcp' command.

:>I am not suggesting that S@H allow Compaq to compile new clients and just
:>bung them out without testing!!  I am suggesting that if S@H are too busy to
:>put the new clients on their web page that they allow Compaq to distribute
:>ONLY those clients which have ALREADY been accepted by S@H until such time
:>as S@H are able to get their own distibution mechanism sorted out. 

: Why?  Why the hell do you care so much?

Why do I care?  I thought that we had already established that I am a
shallow individual who is interested only in his rankings in the results
tables.  Obviously, I want a faster client so that I can move further up the
tables!

:>: Yes, but they want to have centralized control over verifying that clients
:>: perform as expected.  That means that they have one person whom they trust
:>: to do the verification, which is a good thing.
:>
:>This is not an issue with the Tru64 clients as outlined above.

: No it still may be an issue.  You don't understand how clients are developed
: by porters and how they're submitted to S@H.

Obviously not.  My thinking (and if this is wrong please provide a detailed
explanation of why) is that if a client has already been approved (and I
have just proved again why this must be true for the Tru64 clients) then it
doesn't need to be approved again and therefore any issue related to the
manpower required to approve it are irrelevant.  Are you suggesting that a
client is approved once to be run by the porter (no matter how many results
that port submits with it) and again before it can be released to everybody? 
Since the unreleased Tru64 clients have now turned out far more WUs that
many of the publically available clients for less popular platforms this
seems a slightly peculiar approach.

Perhaps you would care to educate us by outlining the exact procedure by
which clients are developed by porters and submitted to S@H.

:>: Yet Another Volunteer is probably not a good solution.
:>
:>Nope, sorry you have lost me there.  Lots of people have been telling me
:>that S@H are very short of people and money (which I believe).  If you are
:>short of people and money, then the problem can only be solved by either
:>
:>a) more money (always nice, but hard to find), or
:>b) free people (i.e. volunteers)

: Because b) requires more people to ride herd on them, which takes time
: and usually requires a).  Get it yet?  No, because you don't want to...
: Do the words "volunteer coordinator" mean anything to you?  Do you understand
: why that title exists?

Hmm, several interesting points here.  The most facinating is your phrase
"Do you understand why that title exists".  Do I deduce from this that there
is a S@H volunteer coordinator?  If so who is it please, I can find no
mention of them on the web page.  Perhaps you meant to indicate if I
understood why the title existed in the abstract rather than specific sense. 
Well, yes strangely I do.  I have in my time been involved with a number of
organisations with a mix of full time and volunteer staff (e.g the British
Red Cross).  Your rather interesting thesis seems to be that a small group
of overworked professional and volunteer people cannot reduce their workload
by recruiting more volunteers because this would produce a net increase in
work.  Hmmm, I suspect that quite an interesting book could be written on
this area of resources management (perhaps one already has, if so can
anyone give me a suitable reference?).  Perhaps there is something unusual
about the situation of S@H.  My personal experience has been that most
overworked organisations welcome well motivated and competent volunteers
(and obviously both of the criteria are important) since the work required
to coordinate them is less than their input to solving the problem,
resulting in a net gain.  This scheme certainly worked for the Red Cross and
also for several theatres with which I have been involved. 

:>Finally, I totaly appreciate that the real reason S@H are not allowing the
:>Tru64 clients to be distributed is that their servers and splitters cannot
:>keep up as it is.  Given this, I just don't understand why they gave Compaq
:>the source in the first place.

: We don't know the real reason at all.  It may be because they're worried
: about problems with source code development as all these different companies 
: port into it their own fast FFT code.  You know they could make an 
: administrative decision to stick with the slow FFT code in the vanilla
: client in order to keep the code base simple and easy for one volunteer
: to co-ordinate.  And wouldn't you *hate* that?

Gosh, aren't we getting ajitated :-).  I wonder how many of the science
fiends out there would *hate* seeing the processing running slower than it
could, knowing that faster processing would allow either more detailed
analysis of the data already collected or more bandwidth or more telescopes, 
and that any of these 3 would increase the chance of finding aliens? 

