Johan Schoofs (jschoofs@compuserve.com) wrote:
: FYI: in the DUNIX V5 manuals Compaq specifically advises to use the GUI
: tools and not to modify the config files using an editor. Most of the ASCII
: based config tools have been archived.

: Hopefully this will do away with this "UNIX management with an editor"
: machismo.

Unix management with text editing tools has nothing to do with machismo;
it has everything to do with automating system administration tasks and
remote system administration. It is very easy to automate a change to
a text configuration file (and I mean an *intelligent* change - not just
wholesale distribution of a configuration file, which is better done
with rdist & friends) on a few hundred systems using sed, awk, perl, 
and shell scripts. It is much more difficult (and usually plain impossible)
to automate an equivalent operation using GUI tools. It is also quite
easy to adjust system configuration on a system on the other side of the
pond using text editing tools (if everything else fails, you just pull
the file to your side using ftp, and then push it back after changing
it). Trying to do the same thing with an X11-based GUI gives a totally
new meaning to the word SLOW, even on a high-bandwidth link: the sheer
latency of the round-trip requests on a transatlantic kills you.

Administering systems via text configuration files also has to do with
robustness (it is much more difficult to corrupt a text file in a subtle
and difficult to detect way than to mangle a complex binary file of an
unknown structure) and with fault isolation between different components
(it is much more difficult to damage a bunch of distinct configuration 
files in a single sweep than to destroy a central configuration database
because of a bug in a system administration GUI).

Administering systems via text configuration files also has to do with
flexibility and the potential of configuring the system in the way you
NEED it - not in one of the ways the GUI designer managed to think of.
As a downside, it also gives you a much greater flexibility is screwing
things up - but then this is the nature of all power tools.

If a system administration GUIs are right for you, and can do what you
want - by all means use them. When these tools fail, as they eventually
will, or are inadequate for the problem you face you will have to call 
in a professional to help you out ... but then, this is how it had always 
been, isn't it?

/Serge.P

