I can't resist to stir up this matter once more...


1) as to the gui vs. 'manual' setup

        as long as Digital/Compaq do not ensure that the gui's are
working properly, one should be
        hesitant to use them.

        example 1: setting up networking under cde is just a mess - most
people know this (now)
        example 2: DESPITE 'following the rules' I STILL had to use
bindsetup in order to convince
                              my machine to accept our dns
        example 3: the gui provides some (VERY basic imho) means to set
up a machine as nfs server/client
                              BUT there are no proper ways to provide
options for exported dirs etc etc
                                        see also below

2) after figuring that the gui actually did NOT help in this case I
resorted to the good old manual stuff

        step 1: let's try nfs_intro and nfs_manual_setup
                        result: some overview about nfs and a lot of
cryptic commands which supposedly get
                                      rc.config right and all the
daemons running, but no nfs mount on the other
                                      machine (which is, incidentally
also an alpha running DU3.2d)

        step 2:  let's try nfssetup
                        result: this apparently allows for world wide
open mounts of exported files (I at least
                                        did not see any restrictions as
options but perhaps I am to stupid to see them)
                                         AND (aha!!) it restarted some
daemons (I said restarted)
                                          BUT - no nfs mount on the
other machine

        step 3: let's try it the most basic way, edit /etc/exports, use
showmount -e:  ok
                                            BUT no mount on the other
machine

        step 4: let's try all this under DU 3.2D (ie the other machine)
                                            everything looks ok AND the
mount in the other direction works!

        step 5: could it be that the kernel does not support nfs? No it
could not, since memory-dependent
                       nfs is built automatically into the kernel, but,
well, let's reboot the machine and take a look
                        at the messages ....   everything is ok, nfsd,
nfsmount and some more BUT at some point
                        there is a message about some missing license:

                            nsrd: DECNSR-NET-SVR license not found,
starting NetWorker SingleServer

                        Could it be that a basic Unix utility which is
automatically built into the kernel
                            needs to be licensed without this
'unimportant feature' considered worth
                            mentioning?

                        Let's take a look at those Dec Campus license
paks ... no there is nothing like DECNSR-NET-SVR
                           but there is some OSF-USR license (which has
apparently been registered but anyway)
                            let's try it once more ....

                            Combine OSF-USR ASP-IL-1998DEC03-3336 with
OSF-USR

UNIX-SERVER-IMPLICIT-USER

and wow! NOW everything works!


Thank you Dec for stealing me one morning of time.

        Siggi




