"Marco Benton" <marco@xssnet.com> writes:

>version 3.2 didnt have NFS built in the kernel so starting the daemons via
>/sbin/init.d/nfs start would do the trick.  and i guess you should edit
>/etc/rc.config to make it complete.

ALL NFS clients and servers for Ultrix/Unix ever shipped by DEC, Digital, and
Compaq were implemented in the kernel.

nfsiod and nfsd run in user space, but they execute a system call that
enters the kernel and never returns.  What you may be referring to is
sometime (I think in V3.2) we changed nfsd to spawn several threads that
run under pid 0.  The nfsd process remained in part to keep a socket
open bound to port 2049 and to handle conection requests when NFS over
TCP showed up in V4.0.  All that is still within that one syscall.

Before V3.2, nfsd forked several children and each made that syscall and
thereafter handled NFS requests ala the PID 0 threads now.

The mechanics of starting nfsd didn't change and I don't think we had to
change /sbin/init.d/nfs in V3.2 to accomodate the change.  Well - maybe
the stop script had to learn that it only had to look for a single nfsd.

In either case. all NFS protocol requests are processed by the kernel.
(automount is actually a very odd exception as it has its own very
limited NFS server in user space, but that's a whole different
story.)

	-Ric Werme
