karthik@cdotd.ernet.in wrote:

> I need to know the following
>
>   1. Why is the value in 'tid' different from the value returned by
> pthread_getsequence_np()? Is there any diff between the two?

The 'tid' in the core file header is kernel thread ID of the faulting
thread. Those values you're getting back from getsequence are
IDs internal to the threads library. There's no connection between
the two kinds of IDs.

>   2. Is there any way to interpret the core file and find out the
> location where it dumps core. In HP-UX, I start the debugger and pass
> the name of the core file as a extra parameter. It automatically reaches
> the location when the core dump occured. I tried the same ploy using
> dxladebug. It did a $load and stopped. Didn't proceed ahead.

That's not a 'ploy' -- it's how you debug core files with gdb, ladebug,
and dbx.  Both ladebug and dbx (assuming you have up-to-date versions
that are know to work with the versions of the kernel and pthreads
library that created the core file), are thread-smart to greater or
lesser extents. Dbx only really knows about kernel threads (i.e.,
that TID in the file header), but will should a good stack trace
for the faulting thread. But it won't know anything about the
other user threads in the program that we're running at the
time of the core dump.

Ladebug, on the other hand, is much smarter about those other
threads. It is fully integrated with the pthreads library can
show you stack traces for every thread in existence at the
time of the core dump.

All, that said as way of background, it's impossible to
say why ladebug isn't showing you *anything*. Was the
program built with any debug symbols enabled, say -g3
at the least? Can you runt he program "live" under ladebug?

