"Marco Benton" <marco@xssnet.com> writes:

>i beg to differ...  some parts of nfs server werent in the kernel.  that's
>why 4.0 had so many performance problems with nfs until pk2 for 4.0e.  if
>they mastered nfs in the kernel back then, why did they just fix a
>performance problem now?

The mount, NLM, and NSM protocols are implemented in user level
clients (mount, rpc.lockd, rpc.statd/rpc.lockd) and user level servers
(mountd, rpc.lockd, and rpc.statd).  The NFS _protocol_ is in the
kernel.

What sort of performance problems?  There was a severe problem with
Gigabit Ethernet on the client and memory leak involving duplicate requests
on V4.0D.  Useful performance enhancements went into V4.0D and V4.0F.
A few more in V5.0 (plus support for > 8KB I/O, but only in server code).

If you use AdvFS on the server, it's performance has been improved during
V4.0x and especially in V5.0.

Can you be more explicit (with numbers?) about V4.0x NFS client/server
performance problems?

