Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hello,

I just obtained a Powerstorm 4d60t card for my PC164 alpha running
du4.0d. The installation and kernel configuration went well and the card
worked like the brochures said it would. I obtained the TRU64 5.0 cds
and decided to upgrade knowing full well that TRU64 no longer had built
in open3d support for the 4d60t (drivers available from
http://www1.service.digital.com/open3d/ and are beta for 5.0).  I first
upgraded the firmware. The TRU64 5.0 upgrade went well with my tulip
10/100 ethernet card (configured as tu0) found without a hitch... my
entire NFS and NIS environment were OK too (sigh of relief). And, of
course, the 4d60t card wasn't recognized so the video was configured as
VGA as expected. After installing the new graphics drivers using the
graphical software installation tool, it performed the doconfig. Once
done, I rebooted.

The problem is that during boot up, tu1 is found, not tu0, so NIS/NFS,
etc hang since rc3.d/s00inet looks for tu0. I've booted genvmunix, found
tu0, and manually did doconfig -c SYSTEM to have the kernel recognize
the 4d60t, but sure enough, when I reboot, tu1 is found, not tu0. The
4d60t looks great, though.

I looked in the /sys/conf/SYSTEM configuration file and found that the
information concerning the bus and controllers that used to be there
under DU4.0d and before are no longer there. So, instead of finding
something like "controller tu0 at pci0 slot 9", all I see is something
like "config_driver tu" so I can't manually change it. If I manually
enable tu1 with ifconfig and restart the network, ethernet is back and
happy. Problem is after I init 0, SRM still thinks ewa0 is there and if
I reboot, tu1 is found but tu0 is looked for by rc3.d/s00inet.

Should I simply edit s00inet and replace tu0 with tu1 or is something
insidious going on in TRU64 5.0? Does anyone know why tu1 instead of tu0
is found if the 4d60t driver is in place? Thank you very much in
advance.

--
